SM(482,{"n":"Acts 23","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 23.mp3","d":3127.1,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.92,"Uh just a moment we'll be picking up at the beginning of Acts of the Apostles chapter 23, where we left off last time, uh, much to the consternation of some folks, sort of in the midst of a story."],[34.72,"Uh in fact, uh St."],[38.08,"Paul was just about to speak, and then we stopped before before we got to hear what he said."],[44.16,"Uh as I always say, uh, if you're interested in going back and listening to uh my introduction to the book of Acts from the first very first Bible study, that's now online, and you can go and listen to that to hear it again, or if you missed it to hear it for the first time."],[62.36,"But to get us caught up to where we are right now, we're sort of nearing the end of the book of Acts."],[70.68,"We've got less to go than we've covered."],[73.96,"But we've read about the beginnings of the church in Jerusalem."],[78.68,"We've read about then known as Saul coming to believe that Jesus is the Messiah and joining the Christian movement that he had been persecuting, who we now know as Paul."],[94.84,"And we followed St"],[96.88,"Paul through three missionary journeys, primarily through Asia Minor, what's now Turkey and Macedonia and Greece proper."],[109.76,"After that third missionary journey, he had resolved to return to Jerusalem."],[116.64,"And in so doing, he was warned by just about everyone that that was probably not a good idea."],[123.68,"Because as we saw, there had been trouble in a lot of the cities which he visited on those missionary journeys."],[130.0,"Because, of course, there was a segment of the Jewish communities who he went to in those cities that embraced Jesus as the Messiah, and then there was another segment of those communities that did not."],[141.8,"And in addition to sort of dividing those communities in that way, we talked about how there were also this group of Gentiles known as Godfearers or proselytes who are sort of at the fringes of these Jewish communities, who are interested in Judaism."],[159.96,"Many of them worship the God of Israel, but because of social concerns and other things, they weren't going to go the full way and be circumcised and become Jewish and give up their Roman way of life."],[171.96,"So they were at the fringes."],[173.24,"And so when St."],[174.76,"Paul began to accept those people into the Christian movement without requiring them to go and be circumcised and join the Jewish community first, that caused an additional problem for those members of the Jewish community who hadn't accepted Jesus, as well as the fact that, as we discussed, a lot of the financial endowment for many of these synagogues in these different cities were actually coming from these Gentiles who were endowing the synagogues."],[208.8,"So we talked about how we have foundation stones from some of these first century and even earlier synagogues that have a list of Greek names of all the people who endowed it, they're not Jewish names."],[221.92,"So we know that that was a significant portion."],[224.32,"So this is also a threat for the perspective of those who haven't embraced Jesus as the Messiah."],[231.36,"This is also a threat to the very existence, sort of, of their community as such."],[235.84,"And so they've reacted literally violently to St."],[239.84,"Paul in some of these cities"],[242.2,"And not only in those cities, but after they've thrown him out of those cities, when they find out he's in another nearby city, they've sort of gone and either followed after him to stir up trouble or tried to get there before him to stir up trouble before he arrives."],[259.64,"And uh word had gotten out that word of this and even some of these people had gotten back to Jerusalem."],[267.08,"And so the uh Jewish authorities in Jerusalem who St."],[273.88,"Paul had formerly worked for in persecuting the church, now uh are not fans, and now consider him to be, in our modern terms, a heretic, who needs to be taken care of."],[284.54,"And we saw last time how upon his arrival in Jerusalem, uh St"],[290.66,"James, who was at that time acting as the overseer, what we would call the bishop of the church in Jerusalem, uh, sort of proposed a plan to St."],[299.7,"Paul because there were some people there who were preparing to take a Nazarite vow at the temple."],[305.3,"And uh he said, well, uh, he said to St."],[309.06,"Paul, go and sponsor them."],[311.16,"And sort of publicly sponsor them as they take this vow in the temple."],[314.6,"This will send a signal and show everyone that you haven't rejected the Jewish way of life, that you haven't rejected the traditions and the scriptures and the temple."],[324.29,"You're not speaking against them because you're taking part in this, uh, which uh Saint Paul went to do, uh, unfortunately."],[332.33,"Um, this is at the time of the Feast of Pentecost, and so there's a large crowd in Jerusalem, and some of them there identified Paul as this person who had been this troublemaker and accused him publicly of having brought Greeks into the temple."],[348.89,"Uh we talked about last time, all the significance of that in terms of with the Maccabees and everything, the idea of Greeks coming into the temple, sort of hitting a hot button in their minds in their history."],[362.73,"And so this had turned into a mob uh that began beating uh Saint Paul."],[368.17,"He was sort of rescued, quote unquote rescued from the mob by some Roman soldiers, because the Roman soldiers, of course, were planning to beat him also."],[373.93,"But uh they sort of pulled him out of it"],[380.05,"Um and uh upon uh being informed by St."],[384.61,"Paul that he was a Roman citizen, uh they gave him a little leeway and allowed him to speak to the crowd, uh, but that just ended up getting the crowd riled up again."],[395.33,"And so uh we saw that uh St."],[399.89,"Paul had kind of been packed away."],[402.53,"And now when we left off, we were sort of right at the beginning of of St."],[407.17,"Paul's hearing. Where they're trying to get to the bottom."],[410.99,"The Romans don't really understand what's going on, the Roman authorities, why they're why these people are so mad at St."],[417.15,"Paul. Um and uh being good Romans, they don't aren't going to believe anything Saint Paul says."],[424.95,"So their first plan, remember, was they were basically going to beat beat it out of St."],[428.87,"Paul. Torture him."],[429.83,"They're gonna flog him and torture him until he told them why these people were mad at him."],[434.15,"But then when they found out he was a Roman citizen, they knew they couldn't get away with that."],[438.47,"And so their new approach is they've invited some of the Jewish leaders there, and they've got Saint Paul there, and they're gonna sort of have a hearing to try and figure out what is going on."],[449.91,"Is St. Paul a troublemaker and we need to do something to him, you know, or is the are the Jewish leaders the ones who are stirring up the trouble and we need to do something to them?"],[459.39,"Notice he says before the Sanhedrin."],[461.71,"So is this a formal gathering of the Sanhedrin?"],[468.43,"No. No. This is the because the San the Sanhedrin doesn't have any authority in this."],[476.83,"He's still the prisoner of the Roman authorities."],[479.71,"So the Jewish leaders who are there."],[487.23,"Sanhedrin has 70 members, so it's probably not the whole group."],[490.99,"But that's um these are the elders of the people."],[494.27,"So these are these are the leaders of the Jewish community in the city."],[501.35,"The Romans, remember, wouldn't recognize anyone as having any actual authority, but over the city."],[509.27,"Yeah. They were less concerned."],[511.05,"Most of the vast majority of the Jews are not Roman citizens, which means in terms of Roman law, they're known persona."],[518.33,"So they basically are looked at as sort of like we would look at cattle or other indigenous wildlife by the Romans."],[527.61,"And so the the they find it useful."],[533.18,"Since the other members of the Jewish community in Jerusalem, which obviously is a large one, will generally look up to and listen to their leaders."],[543.9,"They find it useful to operate through their leaders."],[549.5,"But when it's not, I mean the Romans openly embarrassed, beat killed various members of the Sanhedrin, too."],[559.88,"And so it is it is important in this hearing to understand that they're trying to find out what's going on and assess fault."],[567.29,"But it's not just, you know, do we punish St."],[570.01,"Paul or let him go?"],[571.79,"If this disturbance was caused by the Jewish leaders, it's the Jewish leaders who are gonna be punished by the Romans."],[578.27,"Uh-huh. Somebody's gonna somebody here is gonna have to, because order has to be kept."],[585.55,"And that's we've we've talked before, but that's how the whole Roman government is structured."],[589.73,"Your job, and you're responsible to the person above you."],[593.02,"And your job is to keep the peace."],[596.06,"So if this centurion fails to keep the peace, then he's gonna answer to Pilate."],[604.27,"Because if Pilate can't keep the peace in his province, then he's gonna answer to the higher up."],[612.26,"And um so when there's a problem, people start getting beheaded."],[620.79,"Publicly executed. In the case of soldiers, um, to give you an idea of what Roman military discipline was like, we get the word decimate."],[631.03,"Yes. From Roman military discipline."],[635.99,"You know, that you'd have there were groups of a hundred led by a centurion."],[639.95,"And so uh when a unit showed cowardice or didn't do its job, or fell asleep on duty, somebody fell asleep on duty, they would cast lots and pick one-tenth of whatever the unit was."],[653.9,"10%. That's where the desa and decimate comes from."],[658.14,"And then the other 90% would beat that 10% to death."],[664.78,"So that's how they maintained so when they're talking about like going and flogging St."],[671.5,"Paul, they're talking about, you know, this is this is serious stuff."],[676.76,"Because the Romans um consider all this a waste of time."],[681.76,"And so it's just who do who do we have to beat?"],[683.92,"Who do we have to kill to make this stop happening?"],[688.16,"Tell us and we'll take care of it, kind of idea."],[693.04,"In a lot of ways, they were more like the mob than modern governments."],[697.6,"Um they enforce things."],[701.6,"Okay, and we talked about uh one last thing before we start."],[704.88,"We talked about last time how St."],[706.64,"Paul had already, when people were warning him about going to Jerusalem, he had already sort of laid out his game plan."],[713.2,"St. Paul's goal is to get to Rome."],[716.72,"Because, as he said, St."],[718.0,"Paul wants to get to Caesar."],[720.16,"His goal is to go and preach the gospel to Caesar."],[723.92,"Or as close as he can get."],[725.6,"And so we've already seen, and we're going to continue to see here through the rest of the book of Acts, that St"],[732.2,"Paul sees all of these things as he's getting arrested and is speaking to this mob that was trying to kill him as he now goes into a hearing."],[739.4,"He's not looking at these things as, oh, I'm on trial for my life."],[742.44,"I better do what I need to do so I can get off and keep living."],[745.56,"He's looking at these as opportunities to speak."],[750.36,"This is an opportunity to preach."],[753.24,"And so we'll see what he says now."],[757.48,"But you'll notice, as we've already noticed, like when he talked to the mob, he's not really defending himself."],[764.22,"It looks very similar to the sermons he was preaching on his missionary journeys."],[768.3,"You know, he's trying to use this for that purpose."],[771.78,"Because as he told uh those who warned him about going to Jerusalem, he's not worried about whether he lives or dies."],[779.06,"He's got bigger concerns than that."],[781.38,"Okay, so unless there are any other questions or comments or anything left over from before, or uh arguments or debates, we'll go on to uh we'll get started here in uh Acts the Apostles chapter 23, verse 1."],[799.06,"Then Paul, looking earnestly at the council, said, Men and brethren, I have lived in all good conscience before God until this day."],[808.98,"So he's talking to the Jewish authorities."],[813.03,"Despite the venue."],[817.87,"And the high priest Ananias commanded those who stood by him to strike him on the mouth."],[824.67,"Okay. So you can see the level of animus already here."],[832.19,"This is not just Ananias thinks he's committed blasphemy by saying that he hasn't that he's lived in good conscience."],[840.99,"Uh, not even necessarily that."],[842.67,"It's just it shows you the level of hatred."],[845.31,"Uh huh. This isn't just some troublemakers from Asia, who have come down to Jerusalem and stirred up a mob."],[852.28,"This is this is the high priest."],[855.02,"He knows who St."],[856.94,"Paul is. and he hates him"],[859.86,"Right. They're out for him too."],[864.66,"Then Paul said to him, God will strike you, you whitewashed wall."],[870.26,"For you sit to judge me according to the law."],[873.38,"And do you command me to be struck contrary to the law?"],[878.74,"So he's not so much trying to win over Ananias to be his friend."],[884.66,"But he points out that, you know, striking someone actually violates the law."],[890.58,"You know, striking someone for no reason."],[892.66,"And so he says, you know, look, you're already a hypocrite."],[897.04,"You're already a hypocrite because, you know, supposedly the reason you're so angry at me is that I've violated the law, but now you're about to."],[902.48,"So that's not really what you care about here"],[910.2,"And those who stood by said, Do you revile God's high priest?"],[918.12,"So are we getting the picture already?"],[920.52,"We talked when we were going through uh the gospels about the fact that at this point in uh in history, the high priest and his family, the religious rulers in in Jerusalem, owned uh more than 70% of the privately owned land that wasn't owned by the Romans in Judea."],[943.48,"That they had used uh tithing and temple taxation. to confiscate the land of most people that had been given them by God, according to the Torah"],[953.68,"These allotments, these different families."],[955.68,"They'd taken it and were now charging the people to work what should have been their own land as tenant farmers."],[966.16,"And we talked about that when why are they getting so mad at Jesus?"],[971.2,"This this peasant movement, because there's all of this power and authority wrapped up in it."],[977.92,"And that's what we're seeing here."],[979.69,"And that's the point St."],[980.57,"Paul's trying to make."],[981.53,"You don't care about the law."],[984.89,"You don't care about the Torah."],[986.09,"You're here about your power and your authority."],[989.63,"And you notice their response to you're a hypocrite and you don't really care about the law is how dare you talk to the high priest that way."],[998.31,"Not refuse to charge."],[999.75,"He's the high priest."],[1000.63,"You have to respect him because he's this important person."],[1006.15,"Now, where he says he doesn't, he didn't know he was high priest."],[1009.91,"Well, now we haven't gotten there."],[1014.15,"Then Paul said, I did not know, brethren, that he was the high priest, for it is written, You shall not speak evil over a ruler of your people."],[1023.19,"So notice what St."],[1024.31,"Paul does. St."],[1025.67,"Paul turns it around again because he says, Oh, I wasn't aware, okay."],[1030.47,"Because what does he do?"],[1031.59,"He goes and he quotes scripture."],[1033.81,"He quotes, specifically he quotes Exodus."],[1035.81,"So he quotes the Torah."],[1036.29,"So he says, You're a hypocrite, you don't really care about the Torah"],[1037.13,"Uh-huh. You know, how dare you talk to the high priest that way?"],[1044.5,"Oh, I'm sorry, I would I didn't know he was the high priest, quote the Torah."],[1050.1,"So St. Paul's saying, I'm the one here who cares about the law."],[1055.38,"Not only am I not preaching against the law, I'm the one who cares about the law, as opposed to you."],[1063.94,"Well, what I was going to ask is before when Christ was before the high priest, you told us there was this thing where um Ananias and Caiaphas."],[1074.34,"No, that was Annas."],[1075.38,"Annas. Annas and Caiaphas."],[1077.46,"Okay, so that's not relevant."],[1078.98,"Who are father and son-in-law, and there was a period where they were both high priest."],[1082.3,"Yeah, no, this is."],[1083.42,"So this is this is He's a he's a descendant."],[1086.14,"He's part of the same family."],[1087.34,"Yeah. But it's a different."],[1089.74,"It's a different high priest."],[1090.78,"Yeah, it's an Annas was the one at the time of Christ."],[1094.62,"This is Ananias."],[1096.86,"Okay. Similar name."],[1100.14,"But slightly different."],[1101.1,"I think I've been confusing the two for my whole life."],[1106.3,"When Paul perceived that one part were Sadducees and the other Pharisees."],[1111.9,"So he looks and he figures out, okay, we got these people here from the Sanhedrin."],[1116.46,"Some of them are Pharisees and some of them are Sadducees."],[1118.92,"They don't get along."],[1123.52,"the council, Men and, I Concerning uh and resurrection of the dead am being judged."],[1134.36,"Remember, the Sadducees don't believe in the resurrection, because the Sadducees, amongst their other issues, the high priestly family, remember, are the Sadducees."],[1146.85,"And they only accept the Torah itself as being authoritative."],[1153.73,"And we talked about, it's probably been a while, but we talked at one point about why that is and why they're called the Sadducees."],[1159.49,"Sadducees is actually from the root Sedduk, it means Zadekites."],[1164.21,"Because the uh according to especially Ezekiel, but also if you look at the life of David, it was prophesied that the the high priests in the new temple, when it was rebuilt after the exile, the high priests would be from the descendants of Zadok, who was part of the tribe of Levi."],[1183.05,"The Zadok who crowned Solomon."],[1187.51,"And so this was prophesied."],[1190.23,"Well, there's a big problem with that for this high priestly family, and that's that uh when Judas Maccabius had successfully thrown the Greeks out and started an independent Judea, he made his brother the high priest."],[1206.39,"Who was not a Levite, let alone a descendant of Zadok."],[1211.03,"Uh and at a couple of points in there, most notably around 150 BC, John Hyrcanus, the high priest and the king were the same person."],[1223.31,"Which was never supposed to happen."],[1226.09,"So they had sort of not been following."],[1229.05,"Well, here's the thing there's nothing about any of that in the Torah."],[1234.89,"That's all later."],[1237.45,"So they kind of had a good reason to say, well, no, we just hold this part as authoritative."],[1243.61,"And then they also, for PR purposes, called themselves the Zadekites."],[1248.17,"That's where Sadducee comes from."],[1250.09,"Even though that's not who they actually were."],[1253.61,"Okay. So they're they're illegitim.ate"],[1257.18,"But because they only held the Torah to be authoritative, uh, you can read the Torah, there's nothing very clear, there's some unclear things, but there's nothing very clear in the Torah about the resurrection of the dead or even the afterlife."],[1272.02,"Which is why to this day there are plenty of Orthodox Jews, if you go to say, who don't believe in an afterlife at all."],[1279.22,"Oh wow. Now the Pharisees, on the other hand, accepted pretty much all of what we consider the Old Testament and had, and all of the the prophetic books, and had this firm idea that God was going to return to his people and there would be a resurrection of the dead at the end of time."],[1299.3,"Um, and you know, we see that reflected all through like the Gospels uh when uh Jesus says to Martha before he raises Lazarus, he says, Do you believe in the resurrection?"],[1310.94,"She believes, I believe that the resurrection will happen on the last day."],[1314.54,"And Jesus says, I am the resurrection."],[1316.22,"So he's kind of adjusting her expectation."],[1317.82,"But her she had that expectation as a Jewish person that there would be this bodily resurrection on the last day."],[1324.38,"So the Pharisees hold to that."],[1326.22,"And so St."],[1326.94,"Paul is telling the truth here."],[1329.71,"He is a Pharisee."],[1331.4,"His father was a Pharisee."],[1334.2,"And what and he has been going around preaching the resurrection of the dead."],[1340.7,"Right. But that's but he's telling the truth."],[1343.66,"Yeah. What he's on trial for is preaching Christ."],[1345.44,"preaching Christ And he's been risen from the dead."],[1351.58,"But he's sort of thrown a bone of contention out there."],[1358.64,"And when he had said this, a dissension arose between the Pharisees and the Sadducees, and the assembly was divided."],[1364.4,"For Sadducees say that there is no resurrection and no angel or spirit, but the Pharisees confess both."],[1372.16,"And so the two groups start fighting amongst themselves."],[1378.89,"Then there arose a loud outcry, and the scribes of the Pharisees' party arose and protested, saying, We find no evil in this man, but if a spirit or an angel has spoken for has spoken to him, let us not fight against God."],[1393.07,"So the Pharisees decide to take his side just because the Sadducees don't like him."],[1404.27,"It's not that these Pharisees really agree."],[1406.99,"These aren't Christians."],[1409.37,"But the Sadducees hate him, so he must be doing. you know, about Christman"],[1419.49,"He was a Pharisee, and he says he was trying to appeal to the other leader of the temple."],[1423.73,"He says, you know, if if this would be of man, we'll come back."],[1426.01,"Yeah, that was Gamaliel."],[1427.29,"Yeah, earlier in Acts."],[1428.33,"Yeah, yeah, Gamaliel Ac actsts. in"],[1430.25,"But yeah, he said the same in the Sanhedrin."],[1432.25,"And so yeah, so now the Pharisees have decided they're gonna take his side"],[1438.55,"Because he's he's held up the flag."],[1440.57,"He's a Red Sox fan, not a Yankees fan."],[1443.99,"So they all come over to his side."],[1448.87,"What's the significance of the Narose and the scribes of the Pharisees party, implying that that maybe not necessarily the hierarchy itself, but well, there wasn't really a hierarchy."],[1460.79,"It was disorganized."],[1462.31,"Okay. But the scribes would have been the most educated."],[1465.59,"I see. Okay."],[1466.47,"Uh huh. The most educated part of that party."],[1470.55,"Um, because the fact that they're identified as scribes means they're the ones who are literate."],[1475.99,"The way they were educated, the way, for example, St"],[1476.11,"And it's probably been a while since we talked about that."],[1480.43,"Paul was educated in his rabbinical training is you would spend between two and ten years, depending on how old you were when you started, just copying texts of the Hebrew scriptures."],[1498.91,"So one person would stand in front of the room and read them out loud, and there'd be a room full of students making copies."],[1506.59,"Okay. And that's why they're called scribes."],[1510.56,"That's where you started."],[1511.76,"And you did that for between two and ten years before you started actually studying how to interpret them."],[1517.6,"I see. So someone like Saint Paul literally would have had the whole Torah memorized."],[1523.72,"Well, okay. Verbatim."],[1527.06,"In Hebrew have been able to tell you."],[1528.58,"That's why he's so able to quote it so easily."],[1531.3,"Because he knows it that well."],[1533.46,"But that was that was the the beginning part."],[1536.78,"Was learning the text."],[1539.34,"Then you could start learning how to interpret it and apply it and those kind of things."],[1545.34,"So the scribes among the Pharisee party are the ones who have been through that educational process."],[1550.86,"I see. There were other people who would have joined the Pharisee party because they agreed with that way of life, and they would have followed the custom the dress and the right and followed the customs, but wouldn't necessarily have had that whole education."],[1563.44,"I see. Okay."],[1564.6,"They could have just been an elder of a synagogue, but not necessarily gone through rabbinical training."],[1568.46,"Okay. Because yeah, it as we've talked about with synagogues, it wasn't like today where you go to seminary and you become a rabbi of a synagogue."],[1579.92,"Yeah, that's what I was agreed with that point of view."],[1582.17,"So you didn't didn't have to have a prominent position or anything."],[1585.77,"Just you were you agreed with those people."],[1589.29,"Yeah. I'm throwing in my lot with them."],[1591.37,"I see. And then followed that way of life and those customs, which were fairly detailed, all the rules."],[1597.41,"But so it's the scribe, the scribes are the educated ones."],[1600.21,"Okay. And so they say, look."],[1603.08,"And this comment they make about if a spirit or an angel has spoken to him."],[1608.4,"Remember, Saint Paul already, when he was being hauled in and he talked to the mob, he talked about what had happened to him on the road to Damascus."],[1617.12,"I was wondering where they got."],[1618.54,"Okay. So they're again, they're not converting here."],[1622.52,"They're not talking about Jesus."],[1624.52,"But they're like, hey, maybe some spirit or some angel sent him on this mission."],[1629.64,"And if that's the case, then you know, if we oppose him, we're opposing God."],[1636.02,"So that's that's sort of part of their way of taking his side in this dispute with the Sadducees without really embracing what he's preaching."],[1646.02,"Yeah. When there arose a great dissension, the commander."],[1654.0,"This is the commander of the guard, remember, who brought him there, fearing lest Paul might be pulled to pieces by them."],[1663.2,"So that's how fierce it's getting."],[1665.28,"Uh-huh. It's turning into a mini riot among the Sanhedrin."],[1669.28,"Commanded the soldiers to go down and take him by force from among them and bring him into the barracks."],[1675.6,"Now, take him by force means beat, bubble stab, whoever you have to on the way through to get him and extract him."],[1686.64,"Yeah. Because again, he's a Roman citizen."],[1690.04,"Problyab no one else in that room is."],[1691.92,"Uh-huh. And so when the commander says, go ahead and take him by force, it's you get him out of there."],[1699.58,"I don't care what happens to the rest of them."],[1704.38,"But the following night, the Lord stood by him and said, Be of good cheer, Paul, for as you have testified for me in Jerusalem, so you must also bear witness at Rome."],[1714.14,"So this is Jesus."],[1715.5,"This is Christ appears to him another time."],[1719.42,"This time is less talked about than the road to Hamascus."],[1723.02,"No, I was thinking, but wait, why why do I he's sitting in He's sitting in the brig in the Roman garrison overnight, and Jesus comes and appears to him again and says, Be of good cheer."],[1735.66,"of good cheer Why would that cause him to be?"],[1737.02,"Uh-huh You should be happy."],[1740.81,"Because this is what St."],[1741.85,"Paul's been after."],[1743.77,"He's saying, You're gonna go to Rome."],[1745.61,"It's like he's saying, trust."],[1748.49,"Yeah. Yeah, and that, and that he's still."],[1753.85,"We'll see this when we get into St."],[1755.37,"Paul's Epistles, too, but he has seen himself this whole time as being on this mission that he received from Jesus."],[1761.19,"That Jesus Christ appears to him, and now I'm on this mission he's sent me on."],[1765.96,"So even now, where he's not making his own decisions."],[1769.66,"He's in he's in prison and he's getting shipped around wherever they want to send him, Jesus is telling him, You're still on the mission."],[1778.43,"And I'm the one in control of this, I'm sending you to Rome."],[1779.63,"They think they're gonna send you to Rome, but I'm I'm sending you to Rome"],[1786.52,"Just like as we saw at St."],[1787.88,"John's Gospel. They thought they were murdering Jesus, and Jesus says, Nobody can take my life from me."],[1793.04,"I lay it down and I can take it back up again."],[1795.78,"So this is the same thing."],[1796.98,"They think they're you know doing this, I'm doing this."],[1803.86,"Okay. So notice something about St."],[1807.54,"Paul, though, here."],[1808.66,"It's not just that he's smart, but that, you know, he he doesn't uh, as willing as he's been to be stoned, beaten, flogged, thrown out of town, have all these things happen in prison, have these things happen to him and and and endure, he's not seeking it out and he's not suffering unnecessarily."],[1831.75,"So when the the Roman soldiers are gonna beat him, he points out he's a Roman citizen."],[1836.31,"Okay. So this isn't St."],[1838.55,"Paul is not a masochist."],[1840.68,"That's just be of good cheer, you know, you're in prison."],[1843.32,"He's not a masochist."],[1844.7,"It isn't that he just you know enjoys or he's morose and he likes being edgy or something."],[1850.96,"That's not that's not what's going on here."],[1853.97,"So it's it's that he's willing to endure these things because he has a greater goal inside, but if it's unnecessary."],[1860.31,"And so what we see him do here with the Pharisees and the Sadducees isn't again him stirring up this dispute to try and get him out of trouble."],[1868.59,"It's because within that first exchange he sees, you know, this isn't a group of people interested in listening to me preach the gospel."],[1878.39,"We're not gonna have a productive discussion here."],[1881.37,"Already right off the bat."],[1883.29,"You know, the first the first words out of the high priest's mouth are to have somebody punch him, you know, for for having basically introduced himself and said he has a clear conscience, you know."],[1895.45,"Uh and then, you know, when he questions that, he's basically told, oh, you need to respect that, you know, this isn't going anywhere productive."],[1903.76,"And so that's at the point where he's willing to. extract himself from the situation"],[1913.0,"And when it was day, some of the Jews banded together and bound themselves under an oath saying that they would neither eat nor drink till they had killed Paul."],[1925.08,"So they're worried that basically because he's a Roman citizen, the Romans are gonna shuttle St."],[1934.12,"Paul off somewhere out of the city."],[1936.74,"He's gonna get away."],[1938.58,"But this is again the level that this hatred is at."],[1943.71,"This isn't just we respectfully disagree."],[1946.75,"You can go have your church on that corner and we'll have a synagogue on this corner."],[1950.29,"This is St."],[1950.61,"is Saint Paul evil and he needs to be gotten rid of, to the point that a bunch of them would take a vow not to eat or drink anything until they killed him."],[1952.57,"Paul is a threat."],[1963.85,"Meaning they're expecting to do it soon."],[1966.49,"This is serious hatred."],[1967.61,"Yeah. Yeah. Now there were more than 40 who had formed this conspiracy."],[1975.29,"So this isn't just a c, a couple of random cranks.ame to the chief priests and elders and said"],[1978.41,"This is a great oath that we will eat nothing until we have killed Paul."],[1989.93,"Now you therefore, together with the council, suggest to the commander that he be brought down to you tomorrow, as though you were going to make further inquiries concerning him."],[1996.25,"But we are ready to kill him before he comes near"],[2003.65,"They know they can't get into the garrison."],[2006.27,"They're not they're they're they're not crazy enough to think they can break into a Roman garrison and and kill St."],[2012.91,"Paul. But they know that if St."],[2015.15,"Paul is being transported by some of the soldiers, they can and there's forty of them, yeah, one of them can get through and kill him."],[2024.31,"And if there's forty of them, the number of soldiers the Romans are likely to send is not going to be enough to kill all 40 of them."],[2032.68,"And so they think during this transport, ask him to bring him here again for round two, of yesterday and, we'll get him on the way"],[2044.14,"So when Paul's sister's son heard of their ambush, he went and entered the barracks and told Paul."],[2051.58,"Wait a minute."],[2052.46,"We haven't heard about sons."],[2056.22,"Yeah. There's all kinds of folks we haven't heard of."],[2060.78,"He's got a sister in Jerusalem."],[2062.14,"He's got a sister."],[2063.82,"Well, or who or he's got a nephew who's in Jerusalem right now."],[2067.42,"Now remember, this is the Feast of Pentecost."],[2069.5,"Oh, okay. so uh-huh"],[2070.3,"So uh so you know, he may just be down there from Tarsus."],[2074.7,"Yeah. Okay. Um, but this is his nephew."],[2077.66,"Yeah, we we forget sometimes that uh the many of the apostles had families."],[2083.5,"Yeah. And and the the scriptures don't give us a lot of those details."],[2089.78,"Like, we know Saint Peter was married, but the reason we know he's married is that Jesus went to his mother in law's house."],[2095.94,"Yeah. So it's like, well, if he has a mother in law, he must be married."],[2101.04,"But that's the only reason we know that."],[2104.48,"And we know he had a daughter at least one daughter from early Christian literature."],[2110.24,"Okay. But uh at one point, um in his epistles, Saint Paul says, We have the right if we want it, meaning himself."],[2118.88,"You know, he had the right if he wanted to, to bring a wife and children with him, as the other apostles do."],[2125.2,"Oh, so apostles said."],[2127.52,"children. So at least some of them had wives and plural"],[2135.24,"So this is this is one of this is an important thing when we read the scriptures."],[2141.24,"The scriptures don't give us exhaustive detail."],[2146.28,"So you can't assume."],[2149.17,"For example, that just because no one in the scriptures does it tell us that St."],[2153.09,"Simon the Zealot had a wife and children, that clearly he had no wife and no children."],[2161.45,"Um in some cases, as I said, we know from from early Christian writings and early Christian traditions about some of these, some of these relationships, but there's some of them we just honestly don't know anything about."],[2172.61,"don't. And so we just the correct answer is we know"],[2180.17,"Not, you know, that's not that's not technically here in print in the Bible, and so it's false."],[2187.61,"But so yes, this is an example."],[2189.37,"We we haven't are are I mean, are St."],[2191.85,"Paul's parents still alive?"],[2194.01,"We don't know."],[2194.97,"Yeah. Was St."],[2197.05,"Paul ever married?"],[2198.65,"We don't know."],[2201.45,"We don't know."],[2202.25,"Was was uh, you know, is this his only sister?"],[2206.73,"How many kids did that sister have?"],[2209.13,"Is her husband still alive?"],[2211.85,"Did any of those people become Christians?"],[2215.45,"We don't know."],[2217.69,"He hears that his uncle is a prison"],[2217.81,"So this is uh St."],[2219.25,"Paul's nephew is in Jerusalem at the time."],[2223.57,"He hears that these people are planning to murder his his uncle."],[2228.69,"And so he being a family member, can go and visit him inside the Roman gar garrison."],[2235.65,"And so he goes and he tells Saint Paul."],[2239.17,"Then Paul called one of the centurions to him and said, Take this young man to the commander, for he has something to tell him."],[2245.89,"So he took him and brought him to the commander and said, Paul the prisoner, called me to him and asked me to bring this young man to you."],[2253.01,"He has something to say to you."],[2254.93,"And the commander took him by the hand, went aside, and asked privately, What is it that you have to tell me?"],[2260.85,"Now it's implied here by the way, this is worded that St."],[2262.53,"is Paul's nephew very young."],[2267.55,"Not just a young man like, you know, he's 21, but taking him by the hand and pulling him aside that this is a kid."],[2280.11,"Probably even younger than that."],[2281.63,"Because at age 13, you were considered an adult."],[2284.99,"At this time in history."],[2292.91,"That's what they're celebrating, even though in our current day."],[2297.65,"You're not an adult at that point yet."],[2300.13,"Some of us aren't adults well into our 30s."],[2302.69,"But uh but uh at that time now that.'s not considered but that time in history when you were 1213, you were married to somebody in an arranged marriage"],[2315.23,"And you were expected to go out and start a family."],[2318.49,"Which is betrothed."],[2319.54,"Yeah. Yeah, she was betrothed to Joseph."],[2321.54,"So that was that was normal."],[2322.9,"So i if he's a kid, this is implying under that."],[2327.22,"I was wondering why the Roman commander was being so gentle with him."],[2330.88,"Because he's a hand."],[2332.8,"Yeah, he's a little he's a little kid."],[2337.65,"And he said, the Jews have agreed to ask that you bring Paul down to the council tomorrow, as though they were going to inquire more fully about him, but do not yield to them, for more than forty of them lie in wait for him."],[2349.41,"him. Men who have bound themselves by an oath that they will neither eat nor drink till they have killed"],[2355.37,"And now they are ready, waiting for the promise from you."],[2358.89,"So he informs the commander what's going on."],[2360.97,"So the commander let the young man depart and commanded him, Tell no one that you have revealed these things to me."],[2368.41,"So he says, Don't tell anybody that I know."],[2371.5,"Because he doesn't want them to know that their plan is known and come up with another plan."],[2377.34,"Scrap that and come up with something else."],[2384.1,"And he called for two centurions saying prepare 200 soldiers, 70 horsemen, and 200 spearmen to go to Caesarea at the third hour of the night."],[2395.54,"So remember, they thought, well, if we have, you know, upwards of 40 people."],[2402.96,"They'll just send a couple of guards."],[2404.65,"So this is, you know, bring your 40 right hoodlums."],[2411.86,"And the third hour of uh the third hour of the night is the is three hours after sunset."],[2417.78,"Okay. So this is also after dark, which would make it much more difficult for a group of just random street thugs to carry off some kind of operation against trade soldiers, especially 270 of them."],[2439.55,"And provide mounts to set Paul on and bring him safely to Felix the governor."],[2446.83,"So he is at the point where this has become serious."],[2455.23,"But we also have to remember he's still a Roman."],[2464.67,"It's like, wow, I really love this Paul guy."],[2467.31,"I gotta make sure he's okay."],[2469.41,"This is again, his job is to keep the peace."],[2472.79,"He's broken up now too near riots because of St."],[2477.67,"Paul. And now he finds out there's people planning to attack. him and the soldiers escorting him in the streets, to try and kill him"],[2486.76,"His job is to keep order, so he says, okay."],[2489.4,"Make an example."],[2490.2,"I can either go out and wipe out, you know, half the city, or I could just get this Paul guy out of town."],[2498.12,"And then once he's out of town, he may become someone else's problem, but he won't be my problem because I'm the captain of the Guard of Jerusalem."],[2505.53,"And the way to do that is what?"],[2508.09,"Well, he's a Roman citizen."],[2511.03,"We send his case to the governor."],[2514.29,"We send it up the chain."],[2515.43,"We send it up the chain."],[2517.39,"And let him deal with it."],[2518.71,"We have a new governor, it's not Pilate."],[2520.96,"Yeah. Pilate is uh dead at this point."],[2522.96,"He didn't die of dead"],[2524.12,"That's it. He was beheaded."],[2526.68,"Oh. Yeah, there were there were um we we've talked before about the revolts before he executed Jesus as being one of the reasons why they executed Jesus."],[2538.76,"There were other problems afterwards."],[2541.96,"And so uh Pilate uh remember um earlier, I think it was last time we talked about he was mistaken."],[2553.8,"St. Paul was mistaken for uh the the fellow who raised an army in the desert."],[2560.36,"Uh huh. Um there had been a series of these issues of sort of bogus messiahs trying to get followers together."],[2567.89,"And uh Pilate had let one too many things happen."],[2573.41,"And so he was recalled back to Rome, and state executions were conducted in Rome uh at a set of steps that led down into the Tiber River."],[2584.53,"There's marble steps that led to the tribal river."],[2586.77,"They would behead people at the top of the steps so that their head would sort of roll down the stairs into the river to kind of make a display of this is what happens when you mess up one too many times."],[2600.17,"Um so that's what happened to Pilate."],[2602.21,"Uh-huh. Uh and he's been replaced."],[2605.89,"And that sends a message then to the next fella."],[2609.05,"You you need to get Judea under control."],[2611.65,"Because this is what's waiting for you if you don't"],[2622.01,"So it's safe to say that this was a hardship assignment."],[2626.33,"Yeah, this was not one of the places where you wanted to be wanted to be governor."],[2631.53,"Well known problem province."],[2633.13,"Yeah. Yeah. Um so it was probably not as bad as a border province where you'd have like barbarian attacks and sort of open fighting."],[2644.81,"But uh it was probably, you know."],[2647.25,"Up there. Ones you don't want are border province and then probably Judea."],[2651.05,"Of the list"],[2654.85,"Um Felix, by the way, means happy."],[2657.13,"Okay Yes. So wow."],[2663.57,"Okay, so he says, I'm going to refer this up the chain and get this Paul guy out of out of town, and then I'll be done."],[2672.93,"At least I won't have to deal with any more of this mess."],[2675.01,"And if they still hate him and they want to go after him, they can go to Caesarea and take it up with the governor, and then the governor can deal with it."],[2683.01,"So Claudius Lysius is this man's name, the commander that we've been talking about."],[2688.37,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[2692.13,"He wrote a letter in the following in the following manner."],[2695.81,"Claudius Lysius. Yeah."],[2699.01,"Um, to the most excellent governor Felix, greetings."],[2701.89,"This man was seized by the Jews and was about to be killed by them"],[2708.33,"Now, just a note, because we keep saying it."],[2710.43,"It's always translated as Jews."],[2713.31,"I have to make this note every so often."],[2715.84,"Jew is not an ethnic term at this point in history."],[2721.76,"Jew, the word that's being translated here in Jew is the Greek word Judei, which literally means Judean."],[2729.98,"It's the people of Judea."],[2732.47,"The people of Judea."],[2734.31,"Now, it's where the that word is where the word Jew is derived from."],[2740.71,"So it's not an incorrect translation, but we just need to remember, you know, especially in our age where people are sensitive about anti-Semitism, this isn't a racist term he's using."],[2751.97,"This isn't like he's he's not doing like a race."],[2754.77,"It's the people of Judea, which is your province, Felix."],[2758.81,"They grabbed this guy, they seized this guy, they were gonna kill him."],[2766.73,"Coming with the troops, I rescued him, having learned that he was a Roman."],[2770.89,"Now that's not exactly how it happened."],[2773.05,"No he's giving credit to the room."],[2776.33,"But this is this is the I found out he was a Roman citizen, so I was right there to make sure nothing bad happened to him."],[2783.29,"And when I wanted to know the reason they accused him, I brought him before their council."],[2787.63,"No mention of the go torture it out of him."],[2788.35,"Well, forget that happened"],[2793.2,"I found out that he was accused concerning questions of their law, but had nothing charged against him deserving of death or chains."],[2801.52,"Because violating the Jewish law is not a crime as far as Rome is concerned."],[2806.18,"You'd have to violate the Roman law."],[2810.26,"And when it was told me that the Jews lay in wait for the man, I sent him immediately to you and also commanded his accusers to state before you the charges against him."],[2821.14,"Farewell. So I found out they're trying to kill him, so I sent him to you, and I said, Hey, if they have a problem, they should go talk to you."],[2830.42,"Bye! But this is this is the form for Roman letters, which you know remember in Latin class."],[2839.18,"Yeah. Then the soldiers, as they were commanded, took Paul and brought him by night to Antipatris."],[2850.22,"The next day they left the horsemen to go on with him and returned to the barracks."],[2854.62,"When they came to Caesarea and had delivered the letter to the governor, they also presented Paul to him."],[2861.42,"And when the governor had read it, he asked what province he was from."],[2867.34,"So what province are you from?"],[2870.64,"Because he said, Well, it's the Judeans who are wanting to kill him."],[2873.12,"Felix is the governor of Judea."],[2875.6,"And so he's what do you under?stood that he was from Cilicia."],[2879.46,"Because St. Paul's from Tarsus, which is in Cilicia, not Judea, he said, I will hear you when your accusers also have come."],[2887.54,"And he commanded him to be kept in Herod's Praetorium."],[2892.18,"So he has him kept in uh it's called Herod's Praetorium because Herod built it."],[2898.02,"Uh we've talked before about how Herod, the Herod who's known as Herod the Great, there are several Herods because it became sort of a dynastic name that his sons took."],[2906.98,"The original Herod, Herod who's called Herod the Great, even though he wasn't that great."],[2911.86,"Did a lot of the reason he's called great is he did uh massive building projects all through all through Judea."],[2918.34,"And a lot of the remains, if you go to the Holy Land now, a lot of the remains of his building. projects are still there"],[2923.82,"And he kind of combined Greek and Roman and some local styles from what's now Jordan."],[2932.22,"And he also he had one sort of big innovation which was he built these sort of round these round rooms that were sort of elevated."],[2943.9,"But that was a big architectural innovation at the time."],[2947.18,"And so even though the the Romans weren't all that impressed with him in general or his uh political skills, they were somewhat impressed by his his buildings."],[2960.86,"Which was really his goal in building them was to impress the impress the Romans uh because he he was was you know even though'd been named king of the Jewish people which again for the Romans like being named king of cattle um he had no bloodline right he was a Nabataean he was basically uh descended from what are called Edomites in the Old Testament for desert nomads and so he had no legitimate claim to any throne let alone the Jewish throne so he was sort of constantly trying to prove his legitimacy to someone and be taken seriously by other people."],[2999.82,"He only got ahead by playing one Roman against another and getting favors from them."],[3005.3,"Yeah, he tried to seduce Cleopatra at one point and it it didn't go well."],[3009.94,"So she basically laughed at him."],[3011.7,"Yes The one who beheaded the heads of the little children and Bethelheads."],[3019.66,"Yes, he's king."],[3020.86,"King Herod was the one."],[3022.22,"Yeah. Well, what happened is after him, uh, the Romans, instead of making his oldest son, giving his oldest son his position, they divided what territory they had given him into four pieces and gave it to four of his sons."],[3040.46,"And so that's why the next time you see a Herod, he's called Herod the Tetrarch."],[3044.94,"Tetrarch is ruler of a fourth."],[3047.18,"Okay. So they had even less status and power at that point."],[3053.66,"And squabbling, no doubt."],[3054.94,"And then it went one more generation."],[3056.54,"In fact, we're gonna meet the generation after that."],[3060.42,"Here in a minute."],[3061.38,"Okay. And then after that, it was gone."],[3064.82,"Because once the Romans came in and destroyed Jerusalem and everything after the Barkokfa rebellion, they had no use for these Herods."],[3076.02,"They were pointless, and so they were gotten rid of."],[3099.14,"So he has to be protected."],[3104.7,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3112.06,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3117.42,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.92,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: where Acts stands","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Acts 23:1</b>, mid-story - Paul about to speak when we stopped</li><li class=\"d0\">Intro to Acts from the very first study is <b>online</b> - listen again</li><li class=\"d0\">Nearing the <b>end of Acts</b> - less ahead than covered</li><li class=\"d0\">So far: church begins in Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Saul</b> comes to believe Jesus is Messiah, joins movement he persecuted = <b>Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>three missionary journeys</b> - Asia Minor (Turkey), Macedonia, Greece</li><li class=\"d0\">After third journey: resolved to return to <b>Jerusalem</b></li><li class=\"d1\">warned by just about everyone it was \"probably not a good idea\"</li></ul>"},{"t":123.68,"lv":3,"h":"Why the Jewish communities turned on Paul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Trouble in many cities: some Jews embraced Jesus as Messiah, others did not → <b>communities divided</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Godfearers / proselytes</b> - Gentiles at the fringes of the synagogues</li><li class=\"d1\">worshiped God of Israel, but no circumcision - social concerns, <b>Roman way of life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul accepted them <b>without circumcision first</b> → extra problem</li><li class=\"d0\">Gentiles also <b>financially endowed synagogues</b></li><li class=\"d1\">foundation stones list <b>Greek names</b> of donors, not Jewish names</li><li class=\"d0\">Threat to the <b>very existence</b> of their community</li><li class=\"d0\">Reacted <b>violently</b>: followed him from city to city or got there first to stir trouble</li><li class=\"d0\">Word reached <b>Jerusalem authorities</b> - Paul's former employers</li><li class=\"d1\">now call him a <b>heretic</b> who \"needs to be taken care of\"</li></ul>"},{"t":284.54,"lv":2,"h":"Last time: temple vow, mob, arrest","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>James</b>, overseer/bishop of Jerusalem, proposed a plan</li><li class=\"d1\">men taking a <b>Nazarite vow</b> - Paul to publicly <b>sponsor</b> them</li><li class=\"d1\">signal: not rejected Jewish life, <b>scriptures, temple</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Feast of <b>Pentecost</b> - big crowd</li><li class=\"d0\">Accused of bringing <b>Greeks into the temple</b> - Maccabees hot button</li><li class=\"d0\">Mob began beating Paul; <b>\"rescued\"</b> by Roman soldiers who planned to beat him too</li><li class=\"d0\">Told them he was a <b>Roman citizen</b> → allowed to speak to crowd → crowd riled again</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans' first plan: <b>flog and torture</b> the reason out of him - dropped once citizenship known</li><li class=\"d0\">New plan: invite Jewish leaders, hold a <b>hearing</b></li></ul>"},{"t":449.91,"lv":2,"h":"The hearing: not a formal Sanhedrin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Romans' question: is <b>Paul</b> the troublemaker, or are the <b>Jewish leaders</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Before the Sanhedrin\" - formal gathering?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>No</b> - Sanhedrin has no authority here; Paul is still <b>Rome's prisoner</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Sanhedrin = <b>70</b> members, so probably not the whole group</li><li class=\"d1\">these are the <b>elders of the people</b>, leaders of the city</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans recognize no real authority except over the city</li><li class=\"d1\">most Jews not citizens = <b>non-persons</b>, \"like cattle or indigenous wildlife\"</li><li class=\"d1\">find it <b>useful to operate through leaders</b> - people listen to them</li><li class=\"d0\">But openly embarrassed, beat, killed Sanhedrin members too</li></ul>"},{"t":559.88,"lv":3,"h":"Roman accountability: someone gets punished","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not just \"punish Paul or let him go\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if Jewish leaders caused the disturbance → <b>they</b> get punished</li><li class=\"d0\">Roman structure: you answer to the person above you; job = <b>keep the peace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">centurion fails → answers to <b>Pilate</b>; Pilate fails → answers to higher up</li><li class=\"d1\">when there's a problem, people get <b>beheaded</b>, publicly executed</li><li class=\"d0\">Military discipline: <b>decimate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unit shows cowardice / falls asleep on duty → cast lots, pick <b>one-tenth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">other <b>90% beat that 10% to death</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So \"flogging Paul\" = <b>serious stuff</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Romans see it all as waste of time: \"who do we have to beat, kill to make this stop?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"more like the <b>mob</b> than modern governments\"</li></ul>"},{"t":701.6,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's game plan: get to Rome","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Last time: Paul had already <b>laid out his plan</b> when warned about Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal = <b>Rome</b>, \"to get to <b>Caesar</b>\" - preach the gospel to Caesar or as close as he can</li><li class=\"d0\">Arrest, mob, hearing = not \"I'm on trial for my life, do what I need to get off\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>opportunities to speak</b> / preach</li><li class=\"d0\">Didn't really defend himself before the mob - sounded like his <b>missionary sermons</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Told the warners: not worried whether he <b>lives or dies</b> - \"bigger concerns\"</li></ul>"},{"t":781.38,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 23:1-5 - Paul and Ananias","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:1</b> - Paul to the council: \"I have lived in <b>all good conscience</b> before God until this day\"</li><li class=\"d0\">High priest <b>Ananias</b> commands those by him to <b>strike him on the mouth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">shows the level of <b>animus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just some troublemakers from Asia - <b>the high priest</b> knows who Paul is, hates him</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:3</b> - Paul: \"God will strike you, you <b>whitewashed wall</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">you sit to judge by the law, yet command me struck <b>contrary to the law</b></li><li class=\"d1\">point: <b>hypocrite</b> - angry that I violated the law, now you do</li></ul>"},{"t":910.2,"lv":3,"h":"23:4 - \"Do you revile God's high priest?\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Response to being called hypocrite: <b>how dare you</b> talk to the high priest that way</li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier in the gospels: high priest and family owned <b>70%+</b> of privately owned land not held by Rome in Judea</li><li class=\"d1\">used <b>tithing and temple taxation</b> to confiscate land God gave families in the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">now charge people to work it as <b>tenant farmers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why they got so mad at Jesus' <b>peasant movement</b> - power and authority</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's point: you don't care about the <b>Torah</b>, you care about <b>power</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Their answer: not refuting charge - \"he's the high priest, <b>you have to respect him</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1014.15,"lv":3,"h":"23:5 - Paul quotes Exodus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"I did not know, brethren, that he was the high priest\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"You shall not speak evil of a ruler of your people\" = <b>Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul turns it around again: <b>quotes the Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">you're the hypocrite who doesn't care about the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm the one here who cares about the law\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not preaching against the law - he's the one <b>keeping it</b>, unlike them</li></ul>"},{"t":1063.94,"lv":3,"h":"Q: Annas, Caiaphas and Ananias","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: at Christ's trial, <b>Annas and Caiaphas</b> (father and son-in-law) both high priest - same thing?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>No</b> - Annas was high priest at the time of Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">this is <b>Ananias</b>, a different high priest, same family</li><li class=\"d1\">similar name, slightly different - easy to confuse</li></ul>"},{"t":1106.3,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 23:6-9 - Pharisees against Sadducees","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:6</b> - Paul sees one part <b>Sadducees</b>, other <b>Pharisees</b> - they don't get along</li><li class=\"d0\">Cries out: \"I am a Pharisee... concerning the <b>hope and resurrection of the dead</b> I am being judged\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Sadducees don't believe in the resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">high priestly family <b>are</b> the Sadducees</li><li class=\"d1\">accept <b>only the Torah</b> as authoritative</li></ul>"},{"t":1134.36,"lv":2,"h":"Who the Sadducees were","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Name from <b>Zadok</b> = <i>Zadokites</i></li><li class=\"d1\">prophesied (esp. <b>Ezekiel</b>; life of David) that high priests of the new temple come from <b>Zadok's</b> descendants, tribe of Levi</li><li class=\"d1\">the Zadok who <b>crowned Solomon</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: <b>Judas Maccabeus</b> threw out the Greeks, made his brother high priest</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not a Levite</b>, let alone a Zadokite</li><li class=\"d1\">~<b>150 BC</b> John Hyrcanus: high priest and king the <b>same person</b> - never supposed to happen</li><li class=\"d0\">Nothing about any of that in the Torah - it's <b>later</b> → good reason to hold only the Torah</li><li class=\"d0\">Called themselves Zadokites for <b>PR</b> - not who they were; <b>illegitimate</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Torah has nothing very clear on <b>resurrection or afterlife</b></li><li class=\"d1\">to this day <b>Orthodox Jews</b> who don't believe in an afterlife at all</li></ul>"},{"t":1279.22,"lv":3,"h":"Pharisees and the resurrection","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pharisees</b>: accept all of the OT, the <b>prophets</b></li><li class=\"d1\">firm idea God would return to his people, <b>resurrection at the end of time</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gospels: <b>Martha</b> - \"I believe the resurrection will happen on the last day\" → Jesus: \"<b>I am</b> the resurrection\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus adjusts her expectation; she had the <b>Jewish bodily-resurrection</b> hope</li><li class=\"d0\">So Paul is <b>telling the truth</b>: he is a Pharisee, his father was a Pharisee, preaches resurrection</li><li class=\"d0\">But what he's really on trial for = <b>preaching Christ</b>, risen from the dead</li><li class=\"d0\">He's \"thrown a <b>bone of contention</b>\" out there</li></ul>"},{"t":1358.64,"lv":3,"h":"23:7-9 - Uproar; Pharisees take his side","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Dissension</b>, assembly divided</li><li class=\"d0\">Sadducees: <b>no resurrection, no angel or spirit</b>; Pharisees confess both</li><li class=\"d0\">Scribes of the Pharisees' party: \"We find <b>no evil</b> in this man... if a <b>spirit or an angel</b> has spoken to him, let us not fight against God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Pharisees side with Paul because <b>Sadducees don't like him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not Christians, not agreeing</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Gamaliel earlier in Acts said the same in the Sanhedrin - \"if of man... if of God\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gamaliel</b>, Acts 5; Paul's old teacher</li><li class=\"d0\">\"He's a <b>Red Sox</b> fan, not a Yankees fan\" - flag-holder, so they come to his side</li></ul>"},{"t":1448.87,"lv":2,"h":"Q: who were the \"scribes of the Pharisees' party\"?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">No real <b>hierarchy</b> - disorganized</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Scribes</b> = the most educated, the <b>literate</b> ones</li><li class=\"d0\">Rabbinical training: <b>2 to 10 years</b> just <b>copying</b> Hebrew scriptures</li><li class=\"d1\">one reads aloud, room of students copies</li><li class=\"d1\">only <b>then</b> learn to interpret and apply</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul would have had the <b>whole Torah memorized</b>, in Hebrew, verbatim</li><li class=\"d1\">why he quotes it so easily</li><li class=\"d0\">Others joined the Pharisee party by <b>agreeing</b> with the way of life, <b>dress, customs</b>, detailed rules</li><li class=\"d1\">could be a synagogue <b>elder</b> without rabbinical training - no seminary-to-rabbi model</li><li class=\"d0\">Their \"spirit or angel\" line: Paul already told the mob about the <b>Damascus road</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not converting - just: if we oppose him, <b>we oppose God</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1646.02,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 23:10-11 - Rescue and the Lord's word","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:10</b> - commander, fearing Paul will be <b>pulled to pieces</b>, orders soldiers to take him <b>by force</b> into the barracks</li><li class=\"d1\">\"mini riot among the Sanhedrin\"</li><li class=\"d1\">by force = <b>beat, stab, whoever</b> on the way</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul is the only <b>Roman citizen</b> in the room; commander: \"I don't care what happens to the rest\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:11</b> - next night <b>the Lord stood by him</b>: \"Be of good cheer, Paul... as you have testified in Jerusalem, so you must <b>bear witness at Rome</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ appears again - <b>less talked about</b> than Damascus road</li></ul>"},{"t":1723.02,"lv":2,"h":"Why \"be of good cheer\"?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: sitting in the brig - why would that cheer him?</li><li class=\"d0\">Because it's what Paul's <b>after</b>: \"You're going to <b>Rome</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul sees himself on <b>the mission</b> from Jesus all along</li><li class=\"d1\">even when not making his own decisions, shipped wherever they want</li><li class=\"d1\">\"they think they're sending you to Rome, <b>I'm</b> sending you to Rome\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>John's Gospel</b>: they think they're murdering Jesus; \"<b>nobody takes my life</b>; I lay it down\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul is <b>not a masochist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">willing to endure stoning, beating, prison for the <b>greater goal</b>, not seeking suffering</li><li class=\"d1\">flogging → <b>points out citizenship</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pharisee/Sadducee move wasn't stirring dispute to save himself</li><li class=\"d1\">saw <b>no productive discussion</b> here: first thing from the high priest is a punch</li><li class=\"d1\">so extracts himself</li></ul>"},{"t":1913.0,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 23:12-22 - The plot to kill Paul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:12</b> - at day, some Jews bound themselves by <b>oath: neither eat nor drink</b> till they killed Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">fear: as citizen, Romans will <b>shuttle him out</b> of the city</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"respectfully disagree\" - \"<b>church on that corner, synagogue on this one</b>\" - but Paul is evil, must be gotten rid of</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>More than 40</b> in the conspiracy - not a couple of cranks</li><li class=\"d0\">Go to chief priests and elders: ask the commander to bring Paul down <b>tomorrow</b>, as if for further inquiry - \"we are ready to <b>kill him before he comes near</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't break into a <b>Roman garrison</b>; but a transport is vulnerable</li><li class=\"d1\">40 of them vs a few soldiers - they'd not kill all 40</li><li class=\"d1\">\"round two\" of yesterday - <b>ambush on the way</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2044.14,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's nephew; families of the apostles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>23:16</b> - <b>Paul's sister's son</b> heard of the ambush, entered the barracks, told Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: we haven't heard of any <b>sister</b>!</li><li class=\"d1\">Feast of Pentecost - nephew may be down from <b>Tarsus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We forget <b>apostles had families</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Peter: we know he's married only because Jesus went to his <b>mother-in-law's</b> house; a <b>daughter</b> from early Christian literature</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: \"we have the <b>right</b> to bring a wife and children, as the other apostles\" → at least some had wives and children</li><li class=\"d0\">Scriptures don't give <b>exhaustive detail</b> - can't assume no wife or children because none mentioned (e.g. <b>Simon the Zealot</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">some known from early writings and traditions; others \"we honestly don't know\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's <b>parents</b> alive? ever <b>married</b>? other siblings? did they become Christians? - \"we don't know\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2239.17,"lv":3,"h":"23:17-22 - Nephew tells the commander","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Nephew, as family, can <b>visit inside the garrison</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul calls a centurion: take this young man to the commander</li><li class=\"d0\">Commander <b>takes him by the hand</b>, aside, asks privately</li><li class=\"d1\">worded as a <b>kid</b>, probably younger than 21</li><li class=\"d1\">age <b>13</b> = adult then: arranged marriage, start a family; Mary <b>betrothed</b> to Joseph</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: why so gentle? - \"he's a little kid\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Nephew reports: Jews will ask you to bring Paul down tomorrow, <b>do not yield</b> - <b>40+</b> men under oath</li><li class=\"d0\">Commander: \"<b>Tell no one</b> you have revealed these things\"</li><li class=\"d1\">so they don't scrap the plan and <b>come up with another</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2384.1,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 23:23-24 - The escort to Caesarea","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Two centurions: prepare <b>200 soldiers, 70 horsemen, 200 spearmen</b> to go to <b>Caesarea</b> at the <b>third hour of the night</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They expected a couple of guards - now <b>270</b> against \"40 hoodlums\"</li><li class=\"d1\">third hour = three hours after sunset → <b>after dark</b>, hard for street thugs against trained soldiers</li><li class=\"d0\">Mounts for Paul, bring him safely to <b>Felix the governor</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2446.83,"lv":3,"h":"Why the commander does this","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not \"I really love this Paul guy\" - he's still a <b>Roman</b></li><li class=\"d0\">His job = <b>keep order</b>; two near-riots already because of Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">Options: wipe out half the city, or <b>get Paul out of town</b></li><li class=\"d1\">out of town = <b>someone else's problem</b>; he's captain of the Guard of Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d0\">Way to do it: Roman citizen → <b>send case up the chain</b> to the governor</li></ul>"},{"t":2518.71,"lv":2,"h":"New governor; what happened to Pilate","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">New governor - <b>not Pilate</b>; Pilate is <b>dead</b>, <b>beheaded</b></li><li class=\"d1\">earlier revolts, then \"bogus messiahs\" gathering followers - Paul was mistaken for the <b>desert army</b> man</li><li class=\"d1\">\"let one too many things happen\" → recalled to Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">state executions at <b>marble steps into the Tiber</b> - head rolls down into the river</li><li class=\"d0\">Message to the next man: <b>get Judea under control</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>hardship assignment</b>, \"well known problem province\"</li><li class=\"d1\">worse: <b>border provinces</b> (barbarian attacks), then Judea</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Felix</b> means \"<b>happy</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2663.57,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 23:25-30 - Lysias's letter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Commander <b>Claudius Lysias</b> writes to \"most excellent governor Felix, greetings\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"seized by the Jews, about to be killed\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Standard <b>form of Roman letters</b> - \"remember in Latin class\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2708.33,"lv":3,"h":"\"Jews\" = Judeans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Always translated \"Jews\" - he makes this note every so often</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jew is not an ethnic term</b> at this point</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek <i>Ioudaioi</i> = <b>Judean</b>, people of Judea</li><li class=\"d1\">where the English word derives; not incorrect, but <b>not a racist term</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Here: the people of <b>your province</b>, Felix</li></ul>"},{"t":2766.73,"lv":3,"h":"Lysias spins the story","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Coming with the troops I <b>rescued</b> him, having learned he was a <b>Roman</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not exactly how it happened - <b>giving credit to Rome</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Brought him before their council - <b>no mention</b> of \"torture it out of him\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Found: questions of <b>their law</b>, nothing deserving <b>death or chains</b></li><li class=\"d1\">violating Jewish law is <b>not a crime</b> to Rome - need to violate <b>Roman</b> law</li><li class=\"d0\">Sent him to you; accusers to state charges before you - \"<b>Farewell</b>\" = \"bye!\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2839.18,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 23:31-35 - Antipatris, Caesarea, Herod's Praetorium","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Soldiers take Paul <b>by night</b> to <b>Antipatris</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Next day horsemen go on with him; infantry return to <b>barracks</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Delivered letter to governor, presented Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">Felix asks: <b>what province</b>? - he's governor of Judea, Judeans want him dead</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul from <b>Tarsus</b>, in <b>Cilicia</b>, not Judea</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I will hear you when your accusers also have come\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Kept in <b>Herod's Praetorium</b> - built by Herod</li></ul>"},{"t":2892.18,"lv":2,"h":"Herod the Great and his sons","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Several Herods - <b>dynastic name</b> taken by his sons</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Great\" for <b>massive building projects</b> across Judea</li><li class=\"d1\">remains still there in the Holy Land</li><li class=\"d1\">blended <b>Greek, Roman</b>, local styles from what's now <b>Jordan</b></li><li class=\"d1\">big innovation: <b>round elevated rooms</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Goal = <b>impress the Romans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">named king of the Jews - for Rome \"like being named <b>king of cattle</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no bloodline: <b>Nabataean</b>, descended from <b>Edomites</b>, desert nomads - no legitimate claim to any throne</li><li class=\"d1\">got ahead by <b>playing one Roman against another</b>; tried to seduce <b>Cleopatra</b>, she laughed</li><li class=\"d0\">The one who <b>killed the children at Bethlehem</b></li><li class=\"d0\">After him: Romans split territory into <b>four</b> → Herod the <b>Tetrarch</b> (ruler of a fourth) - even less status and power</li><li class=\"d0\">One more generation - \"we're going to meet\" that one shortly; then gone after the <b>Bar Kokhba</b> rebellion - <b>pointless</b></li></ul>"}]});
